<?xml version="1.0"?>
<rdf:RDF
    xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
    xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
    xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
    xml:base="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features">

  <owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features">
    <rdfs:comment>Sub-national geographic KIND classes, additive to
geography_ontology.owl.

WHY THIS EXISTS. geography_ontology.owl models countries and regions as
owl:Class (deliberately -- its own comment says "Document-parsed mentions of
this geography can be instantiated as members of this class"), giving
GeographicEntity -> Country | Region | Continent and 251 country classes
beneath them. What it has no class for is anything SMALLER than a country.

The consequence, measured on two corpora: entity extraction had no class
denoting "a city", "a canal" or "a rock formation", so it typed each place to
the nearest thing it was offered -- the containing country or region. Eleven
Israeli cities (Tel Aviv, Beer Sheva, Sderot...) were typed to the class
"Israel"; the Panama and Suez Canals were typed "Southern Europe"; the
Anadarko, Bakken, Delaware and Hugoton basins were typed
"ElectricityGenerationFacility" for want of anywhere better.

Adding the missing KINDS fixes this without touching the country-as-class
design: Tel Aviv is a City located in Israel, not an instance of Israel.
    </rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Ontology>

  <!-- Populated place -->
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#PopulatedPlace">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography#GeographicEntity"/>
    <rdfs:label>PopulatedPlace</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>A named human settlement of any size.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#City">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#PopulatedPlace"/>
    <rdfs:label>City</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>A city or town. Instances are named cities such as Tel Aviv, Des Moines or Chicago.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Class>

  <!-- Sub-national administrative divisions -->
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#AdministrativeArea">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography#GeographicEntity"/>
    <rdfs:label>AdministrativeArea</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>A sub-national administrative division: a state, province, county, territory or district. Instances include California, Alberta and British Columbia.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Class>

  <!-- Physical features -->
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#Landform">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography#GeographicEntity"/>
    <rdfs:label>Landform</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>A natural physical feature of the earth's surface.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#GeologicalFormation">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#Landform"/>
    <rdfs:label>GeologicalFormation</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>A named geological basin, formation, shale or play. Instances include the Anadarko Basin, the Bakken Formation and the Permian Basin. NOT a facility -- nothing is generated or manufactured here.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#WaterBody">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#Landform"/>
    <rdfs:label>WaterBody</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>A sea, ocean, lake, bay, gulf or river.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#Waterway">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#Landform"/>
    <rdfs:label>Waterway</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment>A navigable channel, canal or strait. Instances include the Panama Canal, the Suez Canal and the Strait of Hormuz. A waterway is NOT a region.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:Class>

  <!-- Where a place sits -->
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography-features#locatedInCountry">
    <rdfs:label>locatedInCountry</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography#GeographicEntity"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://veerla-ramrao.ai/ontology/geography#Country"/>
    <rdfs:comment>Ties a sub-national place to its country. This is the correct way to say "Tel Aviv is in Israel" -- as a relationship, not by typing Tel Aviv as an instance of Israel.</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>

</rdf:RDF>
